-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb', 'rg12-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Slowest Pioneers$q$, $q$Walk across almost any bare boulder and you will find it decorated with crusty patches of orange, gray, or pale green. Most people glance past them, but these patches, called lichens, are among the most remarkable partnerships in nature. A lichen is not a single organism. It is a fungus and an alga, or sometimes a type of bacterium, living together so closely that they behave like one body.

The arrangement works because each partner supplies what the other lacks. The alga captures sunlight and makes sugars, while the fungus builds a tough structure that holds water and shelters its companion from drying winds. Neither could survive alone on a sunbaked rock, yet together they settle places that seem to offer nothing at all.

Lichens pay for this toughness with time. Some species spread only a millimeter or two each year, so a patch the size of a dinner plate may be older than the town around it. Their slowness is, paradoxically, the secret of their success. Because they need so little, they can wait out droughts that would kill faster-growing plants, drying to a brittle crisp and then reviving within minutes after a rain.

Over centuries, lichens also prepare the ground for others. They release weak acids that loosen the surface of stone, and as they die and decay, they leave behind the first thin layer of soil. Mosses move in, then grasses, and eventually shrubs. In this sense, a lichen is a pioneer that never sees the settlement it makes possible.

Scientists also treat lichens as living alarm systems. Many species absorb pollutants straight from the air and rain, so they vanish quickly when air quality drops. When researchers find rich lichen growth returning to a city's trees, they read it as a quiet report card announcing that the air has improved.$q$, 'draft')
