   $q$Researchers read lichen growth as "a quiet report card announcing that the air has improved." What does "report card" suggest here?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Lichens grow in patterns that look like written letters.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Scientists hand out grades to cities that plant more trees.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The air in cities is always too dirty for lichens to survive.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"Returning lichens serve as a visible measure of air quality.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Returning lichens serve as a visible measure of air quality.$q$, $q$A report card shows how well something is doing, and the return of lichens shows that the air "has improved." The passage describes lichens coming back to city trees, so it does not say cities are always too polluted.$q$, 'draft', '0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb')
