   $q$The author writes, "Their slowness is, paradoxically, the secret of their success." How does the passage resolve this paradox?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Slow growth hides lichens so animals do not notice and eat them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Slow growth lets lichens spread farther than any other plant.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Needing so little lets lichens survive droughts that kill faster plants.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lichens are not actually slow; they only appear slow to people.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Needing so little lets lichens survive droughts that kill faster plants.$q$, $q$The next sentence explains, "Because they need so little, they can wait out droughts that would kill faster-growing plants." The passage states that some lichens spread "only a millimeter or two each year," so they really are slow.$q$, 'draft', '0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb')
