  ('55494ccc-390a-55b8-92d4-c79a2a947ef9', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Lichens dry out and then begin "reviving within minutes after a rain." Why is "reviving" more precise here than a near synonym such as "growing"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Reviving\" suggests a return to activity after seeming lifeless.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Reviving\" and \"growing\" mean the same, so either word would fit.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Reviving\" means the lichen dies completely and a new one forms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"\"Reviving\" means the lichen is growing faster than it ever has.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-intensity"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Reviving" suggests a return to activity after seeming lifeless.$q$, $q$The lichen had dried "to a brittle crisp," so "reviving" captures coming back from an apparently dead state, which "growing" does not. A brand new lichen does not form within minutes; the same one returns to life.$q$, 'draft', '0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb')
