   $q$The coach says, "A boat goes fastest when no one is trying to be the engine." What does this paradox suggest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Only the coxswain should decide how hard each rower should pull.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Rowing boats are faster when they have no motor attached at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Speed comes from the crew moving together, not from one rower's power.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The fastest crews are the ones whose rowers stop trying very hard.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal-paradox"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Speed comes from the crew moving together, not from one rower's power.$q$, $q$The boat runs once Tomas matches "her blade to the instant," showing that unity, not individual force, drives speed. The coach asks for timing, not a lack of effort, so reading the line as "stop trying" misses its point.$q$, 'draft', '82ef284f-3030-5958-9564-1bbb52a0a775')
