-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b4a954b-e234-57ca-8bbc-c282b017bb02', 'rg12-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Every Town Kept Its Own Clock$q$, $q$Before the late nineteenth century, time in most of the world was a local affair. Each town set its clocks by the sun, declaring noon at the moment the sun stood highest over its own main street. Because the sun appears to move steadily westward across the sky, noon in one town arrived a few minutes earlier than noon in a town fifty miles to the west. For people who rarely traveled far, this hardly mattered.

Railroads changed that. A train could cross dozens of local noons in a single afternoon, and each railroad company tended to keep its own official time, usually based on the clock in its headquarters city. Large stations sometimes displayed several clocks side by side, each one insisting on a different hour. A traveler changing trains faced a forest of conflicting numbers, and timetables printed in one city could be nearly useless in the next.

The solution, adopted by railroads in North America in 1883 and spread around the world in the decades that followed, was to divide the globe into broad zones. Within each zone, every clock would show the same time, and neighboring zones would differ by a full hour. The change asked towns to give up a little accuracy in exchange for a great deal of agreement.

Not everyone welcomed it. Some residents complained that their noon had been stolen, since the official midday no longer matched the sun overhead. Local newspapers printed letters grumbling about the new "railroad time." Yet within a generation, the system became so ordinary that few people could imagine living without it.

Today, the zones are so familiar that they seem as natural as the sunrise. They are, in fact, one of the most successful agreements people have ever made, an invisible grid that quietly organizes billions of daily schedules.$q$, 'draft')
