  ('a5ad4466-a239-59a0-82f7-077ec593f7dd', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The author calls compression "a small act of deception" that "you would almost certainly approve of." Why does the author describe it this way?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Phone makers secretly sell your photos without telling you.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The software asks permission before it saves each new picture.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Compressed photos are so poor that most people delete them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The saved photo is not an exact record, but the trade benefits you.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The saved photo is not an exact record, but the trade benefits you.$q$, $q$Compression discards real data, a kind of deception, yet it lets you store "thousands of images" that look "nearly identical," which is why you would approve. The passage says the photos look nearly the same, not poor.$q$, 'draft', '823c1dda-3228-5a71-bc05-911744b562c3')
