   $q$Engineers call these flaws "artifacts," which the author describes as "a dry name for what is really the moment the magic trick is caught." What does this contrast suggest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The plain technical term hides how revealing the flaws really are.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Artifacts are ancient objects that appear in very old photos.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-meaning-in-context"},{"text":"The flaws are deliberate effects added to make photos look fun.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Engineers find compression boring and prefer to study magic.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The plain technical term hides how revealing the flaws really are.$q$, $q$"Dry" means flat and unemotional, and the author contrasts that label with the vivid image of a trick being exposed, the point when the viewer sees the "deception." Here "artifacts" means compression flaws, not historical objects.$q$, 'draft', '823c1dda-3228-5a71-bc05-911744b562c3')
