insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('644ac0f9-9f8b-5995-bbe1-d02e8ddd4f9b', 'rg12-figurative-language-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Three Meteors$q$, $q$The astronomy club had planned the trip for two months. They had borrowed folding chairs, packed blankets and hot cider, and driven forty minutes past the last streetlight to a farm field where, according to every forecast, the sky would be perfectly clear for the peak of the meteor shower.

The forecasts were wrong. By ten o'clock a lid of cloud had slid over the field, and the only light above them was a dull orange glow from a town over the hill.

Kwame Asante, the club president, took it personally. He paced between the chairs, checking a weather app every thirty seconds as if staring at it hard enough might push the clouds aside. "This is a disaster," he announced. "The whole night is a total, complete, absolute waste."

Ms. Delacroix, their advisor, was lying on a blanket with her hands folded on her chest. "You've used three words that mean the same thing," she said, "and none of them are true yet."

Nobody had a better plan, so they stayed. Someone found a bag of oranges in the van. Ji-woo Han began teaching the younger members the constellations they could not see, sketching them in a notebook by flashlight, and the lesson turned into a contest to invent new ones. Kwame, despite himself, laughed hard enough to spill his cider at a constellation called The Lost Sneaker.

Near one in the morning, a single gap opened in the clouds. It lasted perhaps four minutes. In that small window of black sky, three meteors streaked across, one so bright it left a faint green trail.

The field erupted. People shouted and hugged and pointed long after the gap had closed.

On the drive home, Kwame stared out the window. "We saw three meteors," he said, half to himself. "And somehow it was the best shower I've ever been to."$q$, 'draft')
