   $q$When the meteors appear, "The field erupted." Why is "erupted" more effective here than a word such as "cheered"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Erupted\" suggests only one person reacted to the meteors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Erupted\" and \"cheered\" mean the same, so neither adds anything.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Erupted\" suggests a sudden, explosive burst of shared excitement.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Erupted\" suggests the field was damaged by falling meteors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Erupted" suggests a sudden, explosive burst of shared excitement.$q$, $q$Like a volcano, the reaction bursts out all at once after long pressure, with people who "shouted and hugged and pointed." "Cheered" names only sound, while "erupted" conveys force and suddenness.$q$, 'draft', '644ac0f9-9f8b-5995-bbe1-d02e8ddd4f9b')
