  ('7f64b435-961a-5ee2-9164-07206aed9077', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The first paragraph calls the rooms "dim," but the last paragraph says a gallery "seems gloomy." What does the shift to "gloomy" suggest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It means \"gloomy\" and \"dim\" are neutral words with the same feeling.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"It shows the galleries became even darker as the passage went on.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It shows the author believes museums should brighten their galleries.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It names the visitor's negative feeling, which the author then reframes.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It names the visitor's negative feeling, which the author then reframes.$q$, $q$"Dim" simply describes low light, while "gloomy" adds a sense of sadness or disappointment, the visitor's reaction that the author answers by calling the lighting "a kind of courtesy." The author defends low light, so the word is not a call for brighter rooms.$q$, 'draft', 'c0a43c5b-a0c3-5bed-9f55-c4629ce0a585')
