  ('3850aee1-513c-5ec1-ab2c-66b525401922', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author writes, "An object is preserved so that people can see it, but every viewing brings it closer to the day when there is nothing left to see." What role does this paradox play in the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It suggests visitors physically damage objects by touching them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It argues that museums should stop displaying fragile objects entirely.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"It proves that light causes no real harm to most museum objects.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It shows that display and preservation pull against each other.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows that display and preservation pull against each other.$q$, $q$The paradox names the core tension: the goal of showing objects also wears them out through light, which conservators "can only manage." The author does not call for ending displays; the solution is rotation and low light.$q$, 'draft', 'c0a43c5b-a0c3-5bed-9f55-c4629ce0a585')
